{"text": " America's spending problem isn't a thousand small items that add up to a lot. It's five big ones. Social Security, Medicare, Net Interest, Health, and National Defense. Those five are about 78% of everything the federal government spends, which means any spending cuts that could actually move the needle have to come out of that list. Anything else is theatrics. Cut spending, raise revenue, or grow faster than you borrow. That's the entire list. And we know Besson's position on cutting spending because he said it very clearly. If anything, defense spending needs to rise. In the same remarks on the entitlement programs, he said, the next four years isn't the time to deal with them. So, not a cut. In fact, the opposite of a cut. Which brings us to door number two, raising revenues. A few months ago, in front of the Senate Finance Committee, he was just as clear with this. We do not have a collections problem, we have a spending problem, and we have a growth problem. Which is very strange. He names spending as a problem, then says now is not the time to be addressing spending. So we're left with one door standing, growth. But growth is not a decision. Nobody can vote for 3% real growth. There's no bill you can pass that produces it. All you can do is build the conditions and hope.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.9, "text": " America's spending problem isn't a thousand small items that add up to a lot.", "tokens": [50363, 2253, 338, 4581, 1917, 2125, 470, 257, 7319, 1402, 3709, 326, 751, 510, 284, 257, 1256, 13, 50567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15463163721279835, "compression_ratio": 1.633846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 0.0011143626179546118, "words": [{"word": " America's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.56, "probability": 0.9291803538799286}, {"word": " spending", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.8, "probability": 0.9960137605667114}, {"word": " problem", "start": 0.8, "end": 1.22, "probability": 0.995783805847168}, {"word": " isn't", 